from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A neutral carbon atom in an organic molecule almost always forms how many bonds?","options":["2","3","4","6"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Carbon has four valence electrons and reaches a full octet by forming four bonds — see ''Structure and bonding''."},{"prompt":"The geometry around an sp3-hybridized carbon is…","options":["linear","trigonal planar","tetrahedral","octahedral"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Four equivalent sp3 orbitals point to the corners of a tetrahedron, giving bond angles near 109.5 degrees."},{"prompt":"Which functional group is defined by an -OH attached to a saturated carbon?","options":["aldehyde","alcohol","ketone","ester"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An -OH on an sp3 carbon is an alcohol; a C=O group defines aldehydes, ketones, and esters instead."},{"prompt":"Two compounds share the molecular formula C4H10 but differ in how the atoms are connected. They are…","options":["enantiomers","resonance forms","constitutional isomers","conformers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Same formula, different connectivity means constitutional (structural) isomers — butane vs 2-methylpropane."},{"prompt":"A carbon bonded to four different groups is called…","options":["an achiral carbon","a chirality center","an sp carbon","an aromatic carbon"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Four different substituents make the carbon a chirality center (stereocenter), producing non-superimposable mirror images."},{"prompt":"When Br2 adds across the C=C double bond of an alkene, the reaction type is…","options":["substitution","elimination","addition","condensation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The pi bond opens and two new bonds form to the same two carbons — that is an addition reaction."},{"prompt":"A stronger Bronsted acid has a…","options":["higher pKa","lower pKa","no pKa","neutral pH by definition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"pKa measures how readily a proton leaves; a smaller pKa means a stronger acid."},{"prompt":"Benzene''s unusual stability compared with a hypothetical ring of alternating single and double bonds is called…","options":["chirality","aromaticity","isomerism","saturation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Six delocalized pi electrons in a planar ring give aromaticity, the extra stability explored in ''Aromatic compounds''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Structure and bonding in organic molecules', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Predict the shape and hybridization at any carbon, tell a sigma bond from a pi bond, and read condensed and line structures. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, how many bonds does a neutral carbon form, and why that number?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Carbon has four valence electrons and completes an octet by making four covalent bonds. A single bond is one sigma bond, formed by orbitals overlapping head-on along the bond axis. A double bond is one sigma plus one pi bond, where a pi bond comes from sideways overlap of two p orbitals above and below the axis; a triple bond is one sigma plus two pi bonds. To match its geometry, carbon mixes its orbitals: four single bonds use four sp3 hybrids pointing to the corners of a tetrahedron (about 109.5 degrees); a carbon in one double bond uses three sp2 hybrids in a flat trigonal plane (about 120 degrees) with a leftover p orbital for the pi bond; a carbon in a triple bond uses two sp hybrids in a straight line (180 degrees) with two leftover p orbitals. Electronegativity differences make some bonds polar: C-O and C-Cl pull electron density toward the more electronegative atom, giving a partial negative there and a partial positive on carbon."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Methane, CH4: the central carbon has four single bonds, so it is sp3 and tetrahedral. Ethene (ethylene), CH2=CH2: each carbon has one double bond, so both are sp2 and the whole molecule is flat. Ethyne (acetylene), H-C#C-H (written here with # for the triple bond), has two sp carbons in a straight line. Formaldehyde, H2C=O: the carbon is sp2 (trigonal planar) and the C=O bond is polar toward oxygen. Line structures hide carbons and their hydrogens: a plain zig-zag of three line segments is butane, CH3-CH2-CH2-CH3, where every bend and every free end is a carbon carrying enough H atoms to reach four bonds."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is the hybridization and geometry of the carbon in CH3-Cl? 2) How many sigma and how many pi bonds are in ethene, CH2=CH2? 3) In CO2, O=C=O, what is the hybridization of carbon? 4) Which bond is more polar, C-C or C-O? 5) How many hydrogens are on the middle carbon of propane, CH3-CH2-CH3? Answers: 1) sp3, tetrahedral (four single bonds). 2) Five sigma bonds (four C-H and one C-C) and one pi bond. 3) sp, because carbon sits between two double bonds in a linear arrangement. 4) C-O, because oxygen is much more electronegative than carbon. 5) Two, since it already bonds to two carbons."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling a double bond ''two sigma bonds'' — it is one sigma plus one pi. Mistake: forgetting the implied hydrogens in a line structure, which makes carbons look like they have fewer than four bonds. Exit: (a) What geometry does an sp2 carbon have? (b) How many pi bonds are in a triple bond? Answers: trigonal planar, about 120 degrees; two."}]}'::jsonb, 'Hybridization sets geometry: sp3 is tetrahedral (about 109.5 degrees), sp2 is trigonal planar (about 120 degrees), sp is linear (180 degrees).', 14),
  (3, 'Functional groups and nomenclature', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognize the common functional groups by their atoms and name simple molecules with IUPAC rules. Warm-up: what family does an -OH group on a saturated carbon belong to?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A functional group is the reactive part of a molecule; the same group behaves the same way across different molecules, which is why we organize the whole subject around them. The core groups are: alkane (only C-C and C-H), alkene (C=C), alkyne (C#C, triple bond), haloalkane (C-X, where X is F, Cl, Br, I), alcohol (C-OH), ether (C-O-C), amine (C-NH2), aldehyde (CHO at a chain end), ketone (C=O inside a chain), carboxylic acid (COOH), ester (C(=O)-O-C), and amide (C(=O)-N). To name a molecule: (1) find the longest continuous carbon chain — that is the parent, named with a stem for its length (meth, eth, prop, but, pent for 1 to 5 carbons) plus a suffix for the main group (-ane, -ene, -yne, -ol, -al, -one, -oic acid); (2) number the chain from whichever end gives the main group and the branches the lowest locants; (3) name each branch as a prefix (methyl, ethyl) with its position number."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"CH3-CH2-CH2-OH: a three-carbon chain ending in -OH is propan-1-ol. CH3-CH(CH3)-CH3: a three-carbon parent with a methyl branch on the middle carbon is 2-methylpropane. CH3-CH2-CHO: a three-carbon chain ending in an aldehyde is propanal. CH3-CO-CH3: a three-carbon chain with the C=O in the middle is propan-2-one (acetone). CH3-COOH: a two-carbon acid is ethanoic acid (acetic acid). CH3-CH2-Br: bromoethane. CH2=CH-CH3: propene, with the double bond starting at carbon 1."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Name CH3-CH2-CH2-CH2-OH. 2) What functional group is in CH3-CH2-CHO? 3) Name CH3-Cl. 4) Which group has higher naming priority, an alcohol (-ol) or an alkene (-ene)? 5) Name the four-carbon straight-chain alkane. Answers: 1) butan-1-ol (four-carbon chain ending in -OH). 2) an aldehyde (the -CHO group). 3) chloromethane. 4) the alcohol; -ol outranks -ene, so the -OH gets the suffix and the lower locant. 5) butane."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: not choosing the longest chain, which gives a wrong stem name. Mistake: numbering from the wrong end and reporting higher locants than necessary. Exit: (a) What is the suffix for a ketone? (b) In CH3-CH2-OH, name the compound. Answers: -one; ethanol (ethan-1-ol)."}]}'::jsonb, 'In IUPAC names, the highest-priority functional group is the suffix and gets the lowest possible locant; everything else becomes a prefix.', 15),
  (4, 'Isomerism and stereochemistry (chirality)', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Tell constitutional isomers from stereoisomers, spot a chirality center, and decide whether a molecule is chiral. Warm-up: C4H10 can be drawn two ways — what makes them different molecules?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Isomers share a molecular formula but differ somehow. Constitutional (structural) isomers differ in which atom is bonded to which — butane (a straight chain) versus 2-methylpropane (a branched chain) are both C4H10. Stereoisomers have the same connectivity but a different arrangement in space. Two kinds matter here. First, cis/trans (E/Z) isomers arise at a C=C double bond, which cannot rotate: cis-2-butene has both methyl groups on the same side, trans-2-butene on opposite sides. Second, enantiomers arise from a chirality center: a carbon bonded to four different groups. A molecule with one chirality center is chiral, meaning its mirror image cannot be superimposed on it, just like left and right hands. Chiral molecules rotate plane-polarized light and are said to be optically active. A molecule that contains an internal mirror plane is achiral even if it has stereocenters (a meso compound). Conformers are a softer case: the same molecule caught in different rotations about single bonds, which interconvert freely."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Butanol shows constitutional isomerism: butan-1-ol (OH at the end) and butan-2-ol (OH on the second carbon) are different compounds. 2-Butanol, CH3-CH(OH)-CH2-CH3, has a central carbon bonded to H, OH, CH3, and CH2CH3 — four different groups — so it is a chirality center and 2-butanol is chiral. Propan-2-ol, CH3-CH(OH)-CH3, has that carbon bonded to two identical CH3 groups, so it is not a chirality center and the molecule is achiral. For the double bond, 2-butene exists as cis and trans forms because the C=C cannot rotate, while 1-butene has no such isomers because one alkene carbon carries two hydrogens."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Are butane and 2-methylpropane constitutional isomers or stereoisomers? 2) Does CH3-CHBr-CH3 (2-bromopropane) contain a chirality center? 3) Is CH3-CHBr-CH2-CH3 (2-bromobutane) chiral? 4) Can 1-butene show cis/trans isomerism? 5) What property do enantiomers show toward plane-polarized light? Answers: 1) constitutional isomers — same C4H10, different connectivity. 2) No — that carbon has two identical CH3 groups, so it is not a stereocenter. 3) Yes — the second carbon bears H, Br, CH3, and CH2CH3, four different groups. 4) No — one of its double-bond carbons has two hydrogens, so there is no distinct ''same side'' or ''opposite side''. 5) They are optically active and rotate the light in equal but opposite directions."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: calling any branched molecule chiral — chirality needs a carbon with four different groups. Mistake: expecting free rotation across a C=C double bond, which is what locks cis and trans in place. Exit: (a) What defines a chirality center? (b) Same formula, same connectivity, different spatial arrangement describes which class of isomer? Answers: a carbon bonded to four different groups; stereoisomers."}]}'::jsonb, 'Constitutional isomers differ in connectivity; stereoisomers share connectivity but differ in the arrangement of atoms in space.', 15),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The carbon in CH2=CH2 (ethene) is best described as…","options":["sp3, tetrahedral","sp2, trigonal planar","sp, linear","sp3, planar"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A carbon in one double bond uses three sp2 hybrids in a flat, 120-degree arrangement."},{"prompt":"How many sigma and pi bonds make up a carbon-carbon triple bond?","options":["2 sigma, 1 pi","1 sigma, 2 pi","3 sigma, 0 pi","0 sigma, 3 pi"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A triple bond is one head-on sigma bond plus two sideways pi bonds."},{"prompt":"The compound CH3-CO-CH3 is named…","options":["propanal","propan-1-ol","propan-2-one","propanoic acid"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A three-carbon chain with the C=O on the central carbon is propan-2-one (acetone)."},{"prompt":"Butane and 2-methylpropane are…","options":["enantiomers","constitutional isomers","cis/trans isomers","the same compound"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both are C4H10 but connect their carbons differently, so they are constitutional isomers."},{"prompt":"Which molecule contains a chirality center?","options":["CH3-CH2-CH3","CH3-CHBr-CH2-CH3","CH3-CHBr-CH3","CH2=CH2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In 2-bromobutane the second carbon carries H, Br, CH3, and CH2CH3 — four different groups."},{"prompt":"Which functional group takes naming priority as the suffix over the others listed?","options":["haloalkane (C-Br)","alkene (C=C)","carboxylic acid (COOH)","ether (C-O-C)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Carboxylic acid outranks the others and becomes the -oic acid suffix with the lowest locant."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Acids and bases in organic chemistry', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Compare acid strengths with pKa, explain why by looking at the conjugate base, and label nucleophiles and electrophiles. Warm-up: which is the stronger acid, the one with pKa 4 or the one with pKa 16?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A Bronsted acid donates a proton (H+); its conjugate base is what remains. The pKa is a number that ranks acids: the lower the pKa, the stronger the acid, because the proton leaves more readily. The deciding factor is how stable the conjugate base is. Stability improves when negative charge sits on a more electronegative atom, when it is spread out by resonance, when a nearby electron-withdrawing atom pulls charge away (induction), and when it sits in an orbital with more s character. This is why a carboxylic acid (pKa about 4 to 5) is far stronger than an alcohol (pKa about 16): losing the acidic proton from -COOH gives a carboxylate whose negative charge is shared equally over two oxygen atoms by resonance, while an alkoxide from an alcohol puts the whole charge on one oxygen. Separately, a Lewis acid accepts an electron pair and a Lewis base donates one. In reactions we call an electron-pair donor that attacks a positive site a nucleophile, and the electron-poor site it attacks an electrophile."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Acetic acid, CH3-COOH, has pKa about 4.76; ethanol, CH3-CH2-OH, has pKa about 16. The difference is resonance: CH3-COO- spreads its charge over two oxygens, so it forms easily and acetic acid is the stronger acid by many powers of ten. Induction adds to this: trichloroacetic acid, CCl3-COOH (pKa about 0.7), is far stronger than acetic acid because the three electronegative chlorines pull charge off the carboxylate. In an acid-base reaction, hydroxide (OH-) acts as a base and nucleophile, donating a lone pair, while a carbon bearing a partial positive charge (for example the carbon of C-Br) acts as the electrophile that gets attacked."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which is the stronger acid, one with pKa 3 or one with pKa 10? 2) Why is a carboxylic acid more acidic than an alcohol? 3) What is the conjugate base of CH3-COOH? 4) Does adding electronegative chlorine atoms near a -COOH raise or lower its pKa? 5) Is a nucleophile electron-rich or electron-poor? Answers: 1) pKa 3 — smaller pKa means stronger. 2) its conjugate base (carboxylate) is resonance-stabilized over two oxygens, while an alkoxide is not. 3) the carboxylate ion, CH3-COO-. 4) it lowers the pKa (stronger acid) by pulling charge off the conjugate base through induction. 5) electron-rich — it donates an electron pair."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: reading a higher pKa as a stronger acid — it is the reverse. Mistake: judging acid strength from the acid alone instead of the stability of its conjugate base. Exit: (a) A lower pKa means a stronger or weaker acid? (b) Name the main reason carboxylic acids beat alcohols in acidity. Answers: stronger; resonance stabilization of the carboxylate conjugate base."}]}'::jsonb, 'The strength of an acid is really about the stability of the base it leaves behind: a more stable conjugate base means a stronger acid and a lower pKa.', 14),
  (7, 'Alkanes and conformations', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Describe alkanes and their formula, read a Newman projection, and pick the lower-energy conformation of ethane, butane, and cyclohexane. Warm-up: if rotating a single bond costs no permanent change, why do some rotations still cost energy?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons: only single C-C and C-H bonds, with the formula CnH2n+2 for a straight chain. Because single bonds rotate, a molecule constantly samples different conformations — shapes that differ only by rotation. A Newman projection looks straight down one C-C bond, drawing the front carbon''s three bonds as lines from a point and the back carbon''s three as lines from a circle. In the staggered conformation the front and back bonds are offset by 60 degrees and electron clouds stay far apart, so it is lower in energy; in the eclipsed conformation they line up and repel, so it is higher. For butane, the staggered form with the two large methyl groups opposite (anti) is the most stable; when they sit 60 degrees apart (gauche) it is slightly higher. Rings add strain: cyclohexane escapes it by folding into a chair, where each carbon has one axial position (straight up or down) and one equatorial (out to the side). Bulky groups prefer the roomier equatorial position."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Ethane, CH3-CH3, has formula C2H6, matching CnH2n+2 with n = 2. Its staggered conformation sits about 12 kJ/mol lower than its eclipsed one. Butane, CH3-CH2-CH2-CH3, is most stable in the anti conformation, where the two terminal methyl groups are 180 degrees apart; the gauche form (60 degrees apart) is higher because the methyls crowd each other. Cyclohexane, C6H12, avoids ring strain in the chair conformation, and a methyl on the ring prefers the equatorial spot to stay out of the crowded axial region."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) What is the molecular formula of pentane (a five-carbon straight-chain alkane)? 2) Which is lower in energy, staggered or eclipsed ethane? 3) In butane, is the anti or the gauche conformation more stable? 4) In a cyclohexane chair, does a bulky group prefer axial or equatorial? 5) Is an alkane saturated or unsaturated? Answers: 1) C5H12 (2n+2 with n = 5). 2) staggered — the bonds are offset and repel less. 3) anti — the big methyl groups are farthest apart. 4) equatorial — it avoids crowding. 5) saturated — only single bonds, the maximum number of hydrogens."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: treating conformations as different compounds — they interconvert by free rotation. Mistake: putting a bulky substituent axial ''to be safe'' when equatorial is the roomier, lower-energy choice. Exit: (a) What formula do straight-chain alkanes follow? (b) Name the lowest-energy conformation of butane. Answers: CnH2n+2; the anti conformation."}]}'::jsonb, 'Single bonds rotate freely, so a molecule flips between conformations; these are not different compounds, just different momentary shapes.', 13),
  (8, 'Alkenes, alkynes, and addition reactions', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognize unsaturation, predict the products of common alkene additions, and apply Markovnikov''s rule using carbocation stability. Warm-up: when HBr adds to propene, does the Br end up on carbon 1 or carbon 2?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Alkenes contain a C=C double bond and alkynes a C#C triple bond (written here with # for the triple bond); both are unsaturated, meaning they hold fewer hydrogens than the matching alkane. The pi bond is electron-rich and reactive, so these molecules undergo addition: the pi bond opens and two groups add to the two carbons. Key additions to an alkene are: hydrogen halide (H-X) to give a haloalkane; water (H-OH) with acid catalysis to give an alcohol (hydration); hydrogen (H2) over a metal catalyst such as Pd to give the alkane (hydrogenation); and a halogen (Br2 or Cl2) to give a vicinal dihalide. For the unsymmetrical additions of H-X and H-OH, Markovnikov''s rule predicts the outcome: the H adds to the carbon with more hydrogens already, because that route passes through the more stable carbocation intermediate. Carbocation stability increases from primary to secondary to tertiary, since neighboring alkyl groups donate electron density to the electron-poor carbon. Alkynes add across the triple bond in the same spirit and can add one or two equivalents."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Propene plus HBr: CH2=CH-CH3 + HBr -> CH3-CHBr-CH3 (2-bromopropane). The H adds to the end carbon (carbon 1, which had two hydrogens) and Br lands on carbon 2, because that path goes through the more stable secondary carbocation — this is Markovnikov addition. Ethene plus H2 over Pd: CH2=CH2 + H2 -> CH3-CH3 (ethane), a hydrogenation that removes the double bond. Ethene plus Br2: CH2=CH2 + Br2 -> BrCH2-CH2Br (1,2-dibromoethane), a vicinal dihalide. Acid-catalyzed hydration of propene gives propan-2-ol by the same Markovnikov preference, with OH on the more substituted carbon."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Is an alkene saturated or unsaturated? 2) What is the product of CH2=CH2 + HBr? 3) In propene + HCl, which carbon gets the Cl by Markovnikov''s rule? 4) What catalyst is typical for adding H2 to an alkene? 5) Rank carbocation stability: primary, secondary, tertiary. Answers: 1) unsaturated — it holds fewer hydrogens because of the double bond. 2) CH3-CH2-Br (bromoethane); with a symmetric alkene it does not matter which carbon. 3) carbon 2, the more substituted carbon, through the more stable secondary carbocation. 4) a metal such as Pd (also Pt or Ni). 5) tertiary is most stable, then secondary, then primary."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: putting the H of H-X on the more substituted carbon — Markovnikov puts H where the hydrogens already are. Mistake: forgetting that halogenation (Br2) adds to both carbons and needs no Markovnikov call, since the two added atoms are identical. Exit: (a) What reaction type opens a C=C double bond by adding two groups? (b) Which carbocation is more stable, secondary or tertiary? Answers: addition; tertiary."}]}'::jsonb, 'Markovnikov''s rule: when H-X adds to an alkene, the hydrogen goes to the carbon that already has more hydrogens, so the more stable carbocation forms.', 15),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Between acetic acid (pKa about 4.76) and ethanol (pKa about 16), the stronger acid is…","options":["ethanol, because it has a higher pKa","acetic acid, because it has a lower pKa","they are equal","neither is an acid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A lower pKa means a stronger acid, and acetic acid''s carboxylate conjugate base is resonance-stabilized."},{"prompt":"A nucleophile is best described as…","options":["an electron-poor site that accepts electrons","an electron-rich species that donates an electron pair","a proton with no electrons","a fully nonpolar molecule"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Nucleophiles are electron-rich and attack electrophiles by donating a lone or bonding pair."},{"prompt":"The most stable conformation of butane about its central C-C bond is…","options":["eclipsed","gauche","anti","fully planar"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The anti conformation places the two methyl groups 180 degrees apart, minimizing crowding."},{"prompt":"Adding HBr to propene, CH2=CH-CH3, gives mainly…","options":["1-bromopropane","2-bromopropane","1,2-dibromopropane","propan-2-ol"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Markovnikov addition routes through the secondary carbocation, putting Br on carbon 2."},{"prompt":"Hydrogenation of ethene, CH2=CH2, with H2 over Pd produces…","options":["ethyne","ethane","ethanol","bromoethane"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Adding H2 across the double bond saturates it to ethane, CH3-CH3."},{"prompt":"Which molecular formula matches a straight-chain alkane with 4 carbons?","options":["C4H8","C4H10","C4H6","C4H4"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Alkanes follow CnH2n+2, so 4 carbons give C4H10 (butane)."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Substitution and elimination (SN1, SN2, E1, E2)', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Distinguish the four core mechanisms and predict which one a haloalkane will follow from the substrate, the reagent, and the solvent. Warm-up: which substrate reacts fastest in SN2, a primary or a tertiary halide, and why?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A haloalkane can lose its halide (a good leaving group) in two ways. In substitution, a nucleophile replaces the leaving group; in elimination, a base removes a neighboring proton and a new C=C double bond forms. Each comes in two mechanisms. SN2 is concerted (one step): the nucleophile attacks the carbon from the side opposite the leaving group, so the carbon''s other three groups flip like an umbrella (inversion). It is fastest on unhindered primary carbons and needs a strong nucleophile. SN1 is two steps: the leaving group departs first to make a carbocation, then the nucleophile adds; it favors tertiary carbons (stable carbocation), weak nucleophiles, and polar protic solvents, and because the flat carbocation can be attacked from either face it gives a racemic mixture. E2 is concerted elimination: a strong base pulls off a proton as the leaving group departs, and the best geometry is anti-periplanar (the H and leaving group on opposite sides). E1 is two-step elimination through the same carbocation as SN1. Both eliminations tend to follow Zaitsev''s rule, forming the more substituted, more stable alkene."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"CH3-CH2-Br (a primary halide) plus a strong nucleophile such as OH-: SN2 wins, giving CH3-CH2-OH with backside attack. A tertiary halide, (CH3)3C-Br, in water with no strong nucleophile: SN1 dominates, forming the tertiary carbocation first and giving (CH3)3C-OH, along with some E1 alkene. The same tertiary halide with a strong bulky base favors E2, removing a proton to give the alkene (CH3)2C=CH2 by Zaitsev''s preference. A secondary halide such as CH3-CHBr-CH3 can go SN2 or E2 depending on the reagent: a good nucleophile leans toward substitution, a strong hindered base leans toward elimination."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which mechanism inverts the carbon like an umbrella? 2) Which two mechanisms pass through a carbocation? 3) What substrate class is best for SN2, primary or tertiary? 4) What geometry does E2 prefer between the H and the leaving group? 5) Zaitsev''s rule predicts the more, or the less, substituted alkene? Answers: 1) SN2 (backside attack causes inversion of configuration). 2) SN1 and E1. 3) primary — least steric hindrance to backside attack. 4) anti-periplanar (H and leaving group on opposite sides). 5) the more substituted, more stable alkene."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: expecting SN2 on a bulky tertiary carbon — crowding blocks backside attack, so tertiary substrates go SN1 or E2 instead. Mistake: forgetting that SN1 gives a racemic product because the flat carbocation is attacked from both faces. Exit: (a) Which substitution mechanism is concerted and gives inversion? (b) Which elimination mechanism needs a strong base in one step? Answers: SN2; E2."}]}'::jsonb, 'SN2 and E2 are one-step (concerted) and favored by strong nucleophiles or bases; SN1 and E1 are two-step, going through a carbocation, and favored by tertiary substrates in polar protic solvents.', 16),
  (11, 'Alcohols and ethers', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Classify alcohols, explain their high boiling points, and predict the products of oxidation, dehydration, and substitution. Warm-up: why does ethanol boil much higher than the similarly sized ethane?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An alcohol has an -OH on a saturated carbon. Its -OH group forms hydrogen bonds — a strong attraction between the O-H of one molecule and the oxygen of another — which raises boiling points and makes small alcohols water-soluble. Alcohols are weakly acidic (pKa about 16). Three reactions matter most. Oxidation depends on class: a primary alcohol oxidizes to an aldehyde and on to a carboxylic acid, a secondary alcohol oxidizes to a ketone, and a tertiary alcohol does not oxidize (no H on the carbon bearing OH). Dehydration under acid removes H and OH from neighboring carbons to form an alkene (an elimination, following Zaitsev). Substitution converts the -OH into a leaving group so a halide can replace it, giving an alkyl halide. Ethers have the pattern C-O-C, two carbons joined by an oxygen; they lack an O-H, so they cannot hydrogen-bond to each other, boil lower than alcohols, and are fairly unreactive, which makes them common solvents. The Williamson synthesis makes an ether by an SN2 reaction between an alkoxide (R-O-) and an alkyl halide. A three-membered cyclic ether is an epoxide, which is strained and therefore unusually reactive."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Ethanol, CH3-CH2-OH, is a primary alcohol; mild oxidation gives ethanal (acetaldehyde), CH3-CHO, and stronger oxidation gives ethanoic acid, CH3-COOH. Propan-2-ol, CH3-CH(OH)-CH3, is secondary and oxidizes to propan-2-one (acetone). 2-Methylpropan-2-ol, (CH3)3C-OH, is tertiary and resists oxidation. Acid-catalyzed dehydration of ethanol gives ethene, CH2=CH2, plus water. Ethanol''s boiling point (about 78 degrees C) far exceeds ethane''s (about -89 degrees C) because of hydrogen bonding through the -OH. A Williamson synthesis of diethyl ether combines an ethoxide, CH3-CH2-O-, with bromoethane, CH3-CH2-Br, to give CH3-CH2-O-CH2-CH3."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Classify CH3-CH2-CH2-OH as primary, secondary, or tertiary. 2) What does a secondary alcohol give on oxidation? 3) Why can a tertiary alcohol not be oxidized to a carbonyl? 4) What kind of product does acid dehydration of an alcohol give? 5) Why do ethers boil lower than alcohols of similar size? Answers: 1) primary — the -OH carbon is attached to only one other carbon. 2) a ketone. 3) the carbon bearing the -OH has no hydrogen to remove, which oxidation to a C=O requires. 4) an alkene (elimination of water). 5) ethers have no O-H, so they cannot hydrogen-bond to each other."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: oxidizing a tertiary alcohol to a ketone — it cannot, because there is no H on the carbinol carbon. Mistake: expecting ethers to hydrogen-bond like alcohols; they accept H-bonds from water but cannot donate to each other. Exit: (a) A primary alcohol oxidizes first to what? (b) What reaction builds an ether from an alkoxide and an alkyl halide? Answers: an aldehyde; the Williamson ether synthesis (an SN2 reaction)."}]}'::jsonb, 'An alcohol is classified by the carbon bearing the -OH: primary (one carbon attached), secondary (two), or tertiary (three); the class controls how it oxidizes.', 14),
  (12, 'Aromatic compounds and reactions', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"State the conditions for aromaticity, explain why benzene resists addition, and name the main electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions. Warm-up: an alkene readily adds Br2, but benzene does not — what does benzene protect by refusing?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Benzene, C6H6, is a flat six-membered ring drawn with alternating double bonds, but the six pi electrons are actually delocalized evenly around the ring — usually shown as a circle inside the hexagon. This delocalization gives extra stability called aromaticity. A ring is aromatic when it is cyclic, planar, fully conjugated (a p orbital on every ring atom), and holds 4n+2 pi electrons (Huckel''s rule: 2, 6, 10, ...); benzene fits with 6. Because breaking the ring would cost that stabilization, benzene does not undergo addition the way an alkene does. Instead it reacts by electrophilic aromatic substitution (EAS): an electrophile replaces one ring hydrogen while the aromatic system is restored. The main EAS reactions are halogenation (Cl2 or Br2 with a catalyst such as FeBr3), nitration (HNO3 with H2SO4, installing -NO2), sulfonation (SO3 in H2SO4, installing -SO3H), and Friedel-Crafts alkylation or acylation (adding an alkyl or acyl group with an AlCl3 catalyst). Groups already on the ring steer the next substitution: activating groups (such as -OH, -NH2, -CH3) speed it up and direct new groups to the ortho and para positions, while deactivating groups (such as -NO2) slow it down and direct to the meta position."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Benzene plus Br2 with an FeBr3 catalyst gives bromobenzene, C6H5-Br, plus HBr — substitution, not addition, so only one H is replaced. Nitration of benzene with HNO3 and H2SO4 gives nitrobenzene, C6H5-NO2. Friedel-Crafts alkylation of benzene with CH3-Cl and AlCl3 gives toluene, C6H5-CH3. Because -CH3 is an activating, ortho/para-directing group, further nitration of toluene puts the new -NO2 mainly at the ortho and para positions. By contrast, nitrobenzene already carries the deactivating -NO2 group, so a second substitution is slower and goes to the meta position."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) How many pi electrons are in benzene, and does that satisfy 4n+2? 2) Does benzene prefer addition or substitution, and why? 3) Name the product of benzene plus Br2 with FeBr3. 4) Is -NO2 an activating or a deactivating group? 5) Where does an ortho/para-directing group send the next substituent? Answers: 1) six pi electrons; yes, with n = 1, 4(1)+2 = 6. 2) substitution — it preserves the aromatic stabilization instead of destroying it. 3) bromobenzene (plus HBr). 4) deactivating, and it is a meta-director. 5) to the ortho and para positions of the ring."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: expecting benzene to add Br2 across a double bond like an alkene — it substitutes instead to keep its aromaticity. Mistake: mixing up directing effects; most activators are ortho/para directors and most strong deactivators are meta directors. Exit: (a) How many pi electrons make a single ring aromatic under Huckel''s rule with n = 1? (b) Name the reaction class benzene undergoes with electrophiles. Answers: six; electrophilic aromatic substitution."}]}'::jsonb, 'Aromatic rings react by substitution, not addition, because keeping the ring intact preserves the large stabilization of aromaticity.', 15),
  (13, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The SN2 mechanism is fastest on which substrate and gives which stereochemical result?","options":["tertiary, retention","primary, inversion","tertiary, racemization","primary, retention"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Backside attack works best on an unhindered primary carbon and flips the configuration (inversion)."},{"prompt":"Which pair of mechanisms passes through a carbocation intermediate?","options":["SN2 and E2","SN1 and E1","SN2 and SN1","E1 and E2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SN1 and E1 both ionize first to a carbocation, then proceed in a second step."},{"prompt":"Oxidation of the secondary alcohol propan-2-ol gives…","options":["propanal","propan-2-one","propanoic acid","no reaction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A secondary alcohol oxidizes to a ketone — here propan-2-one (acetone)."},{"prompt":"Why does benzene undergo substitution rather than addition?","options":["it has no pi electrons","adding groups would destroy its aromatic stabilization","it is a saturated ring","it cannot react with electrophiles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Substitution keeps the six delocalized pi electrons and the aromatic stability intact."},{"prompt":"A tertiary alcohol treated with an oxidizing agent…","options":["forms an aldehyde","forms a ketone","forms a carboxylic acid","does not oxidize to a carbonyl"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The carbon bearing the -OH has no hydrogen to remove, so no carbonyl forms."},{"prompt":"The nitro group -NO2 on a benzene ring is…","options":["activating and ortho/para-directing","deactivating and meta-directing","activating and meta-directing","unreactive and non-directing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"-NO2 withdraws electron density, slowing substitution and directing new groups to the meta position."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (14, 'Spectroscopy and an introduction to biomolecules', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Match a spectroscopic method to the structural clue it gives, read a few signature signals, and connect functional groups to the four families of biomolecules. Warm-up: which technique would quickly tell you whether a molecule contains a C=O group?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Chemists deduce structure without seeing molecules directly by using several complementary methods. Infrared (IR) spectroscopy detects bond vibrations, so it flags functional groups: a strong band near 1700 per cm signals a C=O, and a broad band near 3300 per cm signals an O-H. Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) reports on the hydrogen (and carbon) framework: the chemical shift tells the chemical environment, the integration (peak area) tells how many hydrogens, and the splitting follows the n+1 rule, where a signal splits into n+1 lines when it has n neighboring hydrogens. Mass spectrometry weighs the molecule: the molecular ion peak gives the molecular mass, and fragment peaks hint at how the molecule breaks apart. Together these pin down a structure. The same functional groups organize the molecules of life. Carbohydrates carry many -OH groups plus a carbonyl. Lipids are largely hydrocarbon and include esters (fats) that are not water-soluble. Amino acids contain both a carboxylic acid and an amine, and link through amide bonds into proteins. Nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) string together units built from a sugar, a phosphate, and a nitrogen base. Seeing biomolecules as assemblies of familiar functional groups makes their chemistry predictable."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A compound shows a strong IR band near 1715 per cm and no broad O-H band: this points to a ketone or aldehyde (a C=O without an -OH). Ethanol, CH3-CH2-OH, shows an IR O-H stretch near 3300 per cm, and in its NMR the CH3 group appears as three lines (a triplet, split by the two neighboring CH2 hydrogens, n+1 = 3) while the CH2 appears as four lines (a quartet, split by the three CH3 hydrogens, n+1 = 4). In mass spectrometry, a molecular ion at mass 46 fits ethanol''s formula C2H6O. On the biomolecule side, glucose is a carbohydrate rich in -OH groups, a triglyceride is a lipid built from three ester bonds, and a dipeptide joins two amino acids through one amide (peptide) bond."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Which technique best identifies a functional group from bond vibrations? 2) A broad IR band near 3300 per cm suggests which group? 3) In NMR, a hydrogen with two neighboring hydrogens shows how many lines? 4) What does the molecular ion peak in mass spectrometry tell you? 5) What functional group links amino acids into proteins? Answers: 1) infrared (IR) spectroscopy. 2) an O-H group (an alcohol or acid). 3) three lines (a triplet), by the n+1 rule. 4) the molecular mass of the compound. 5) the amide (peptide) bond."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: expecting one technique to reveal everything — IR, NMR, and mass spectrometry each answer a different question and are strongest together. Mistake: forgetting the n+1 rule counts neighboring hydrogens, not the hydrogens on the signaling carbon itself. Exit: (a) Which IR region flags a C=O group? (b) Which biomolecule family is built mainly from ester bonds and hydrocarbon chains? Answers: near 1700 per cm; lipids (fats)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Spectroscopy identifies structure indirectly: each method reports on a different feature — IR on functional groups, NMR on the carbon-hydrogen framework, mass spec on molecular weight and fragments.', 15),
  (15, 'Comprehensive final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A carbon forming one double bond and two single bonds is…","options":["sp3, tetrahedral","sp2, trigonal planar","sp, linear","sp3, bent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"One double bond means three sigma-bond directions from sp2 hybrids in a flat, 120-degree plane."},{"prompt":"The compound CH3-CH2-COOH is named…","options":["propanal","propan-2-one","propanoic acid","propan-1-ol"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A three-carbon chain ending in -COOH is propanoic acid."},{"prompt":"Which molecule is chiral?","options":["CH3-CH3","CH3-CHBr-CH2-CH3","CH3-CHBr-CH3","CH2=CH2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In 2-bromobutane the second carbon bears four different groups (H, Br, CH3, CH2CH3)."},{"prompt":"Ranking acidity, which is the strongest acid?","options":["ethanol, pKa about 16","acetic acid, pKa about 4.76","trichloroacetic acid, pKa about 0.7","methane, pKa about 50"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The lowest pKa is the strongest acid; electron-withdrawing chlorines stabilize the conjugate base."},{"prompt":"Adding HBr to propene, CH2=CH-CH3, follows Markovnikov''s rule to give…","options":["1-bromopropane","2-bromopropane","propan-1-ol","1,2-dibromopropane"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The reaction routes through the more stable secondary carbocation, placing Br on carbon 2."},{"prompt":"The most stable conformation of butane about its central bond is…","options":["eclipsed","gauche","anti","cis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Anti places the two methyl groups 180 degrees apart, the least crowded arrangement."},{"prompt":"A primary haloalkane with a strong nucleophile most likely reacts by…","options":["SN1","SN2","E1","no reaction"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Unhindered primary substrates with strong nucleophiles favor concerted SN2 with inversion."},{"prompt":"Oxidizing a primary alcohol all the way gives a…","options":["ketone","carboxylic acid","tertiary alcohol","ether"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A primary alcohol oxidizes to an aldehyde and then on to a carboxylic acid."},{"prompt":"Benzene reacts with an electrophile mainly by…","options":["addition across a double bond","electrophilic aromatic substitution","elimination","hydrogenation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Substitution replaces one ring hydrogen and restores the aromatic system, preserving its stability."},{"prompt":"A strong IR absorption near 1700 per cm most directly indicates…","options":["an O-H group","a C=O group","a C-C single bond","an aromatic ring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The carbonyl (C=O) stretch appears as a strong band near 1700 per cm."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-000000000027' and l.position = v.pos
);
